{
    "id": "phonology-lab",
    "name": "Phonology Lab",
    "category": "phonology",
    "moduleIntroduction": "Welcome to the Phonology Lab! 🎙️\n\nThis special module is designed to help you sound more like a native English speaker. Research shows that two of the biggest challenges for Francophone learners are **Consonant Clusters** (like 'str' in 'street') and **Word Stress** (knowing which part of the word to say loudly).\n\nIn this lab, you will:\n1.  **Listen** to the correct pronunciation.\n2.  **Record** your own voice.\n3.  **Compare** the two to improve your accent.\n\nMastering these sounds will make your English clearer and more confident!",
    "contrastiveExplanation": "Why is this hard?\n\n**1. Consonant Clusters:**\nFrench rarely puts 3 consonants together (like 'spr' or 'str'). English does this often! French speakers often add a vowel sound to make it easier (e.g., saying \"es-port\" instead of \"sport\").\n\n**2. Word Stress:**\nIn French, stress is usually even or at the end of the word. In English, stress changes the meaning! (e.g., 'RE-cord' is a noun, 're-CORD' is a verb). Using the wrong stress can confuse listeners.",
    "commonErrors": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "subTopic": "S-Clusters",
            "wrong": "I like es-sport.",
            "correct": "I like sport.",
            "frenchConnection": "Adding an 'e' sound before 's' (e.g., 'es-school', 'es-start')."
        },
        {
            "number": 2,
            "subTopic": "Word Stress",
            "wrong": "ComforTAble",
            "correct": "COMfortable",
            "frenchConnection": "Stressing the end of the word instead of the beginning."
        }
    ],
